    </svg>
  </div>
  <div class="cases">
    <article>
      <p>North Sea</p>
      <h3>Doggerland</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Observed</dt><dd>A Neanderthal skull fragment was dredged from the North Sea. A decorated bison bone and later human remains also came up in fishing nets.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Established context</dt><dd>Sea level was lower, and land connected Britain to continental Europe.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Unresolved</dt><dd>Dredging removes precise archaeological context. The full distribution of camps, images, and organic material remains unknown.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p>Taiwan Strait</p>
      <h3>Penghu Channel</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Observed</dt><dd>Penghu 1, a robust human jaw, was recovered by a fishing net from a submarine channel. Ancient proteins identified it as Denisovan in 2025.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Established context</dt><dd>The channel was exposed land during Pleistocene low sea levels.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Unresolved</dt><dd>The jaw lacks an excavated layer, and its age falls within broad possible windows.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </div>
  <footer>Diagram is schematic and not to scale. It shows why discovery context differs from an excavated terrestrial site.</footer>
</section>

